.\" OpenBSD-style concise man page
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt TAC 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tac
.Nd concatenate and print files in reverse
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl b
.Op Fl s Ar separator
.Op Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility writes each
.Ar file
to standard output, last line first.
When no
.Ar file
is given, or when
.Ar file
is
.Ql - ,
standard input is read.
.Pp
By default, records are separated by newline characters.
Each file is reversed independently.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl b , Fl Fl before
Attach the separator before the record instead of after.
.It Fl s Ar separator , Fl Fl separator Ns = Ns Ar separator
Use
.Ar separator
as the record separator instead of newline.
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Display help information and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl Fl version
Output version information and exit.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std tac
.Sh EXAMPLES
Reverse the lines of a file:
.Pp
.Dl $ tac file.txt
.Pp
Reverse colon-delimited records:
.Pp
.Dl $ echo 'a:b:c:' | tac -s :
.Pp
Reverse lines from multiple files:
.Pp
.Dl $ tac file1.txt file2.txt
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cat 1 ,
.Xr rev 1 ,
.Xr tail 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is a GNU extension and is not specified by POSIX.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "vibeutils implementation by Travis Cole"
